---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Portrait from '../assets/img/hanif-author-portrait.jpg';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { BOOK_CALL_URL, EMAIL_URL } from '../consts';
import '../styles/projects.css';
import '../styles/notes.css';

const title = 'Notes';
const description =
	'A browsable library of ideas I have developed through building products, working independently, learning, training, and trying to live an intentional life.';

const entryId = (section: string, title: string) =>
	`${section}-${title
		.toLowerCase()
		.replace(/[’']/g, '')
		.replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-')
		.replace(/(^-|-$)/g, '')}`;

const sections = [
	{
		slug: 'building-products',
		title: 'Building Products',
		purpose: 'Product decisions I have learned through building, testing, and observing users.',
		entries: [
			{
				title: 'Let the outcome define the product',
				body: [
					`I started thinking about this while building a tool for making videos. I had seen another product that could generate many kinds of videos through an agent, but I wasn’t sure what that generality would do for me. What I wanted was much more specific: a tool for making explanatory, talking-head videos, sometimes with a recording of my screen.`,
					`Once I named the outcome, the product became easier to think about. I could remove tools that didn’t help me make that kind of video. The same question has helped me on other projects: what does someone want to accomplish, and what is the smallest product that gets them there?`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'High-stakes decisions need visible choices',
				body: [
					`My first design for Casamo returned one accommodation recommendation and two backups. I wanted to spare people from comparing another long list after the product had already checked the listings against their requirements.`,
					`That design made sense for a low-stakes choice. A booking can cost thousands of dollars and determine where someone lives for weeks. In that situation, people wanted to compare a reasonable set of good options before trusting the recommendation. The interface had to show enough of the decision for them to make it themselves, so I changed the report to return roughly six to ten matches.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Constrain the parts handled by AI',
				body: [
					`The first version of the generation flow for a Spanish-learning product asked one language model to write a story, use the requested vocabulary and grammar, and produce the definitions and explanations. It was too much to do reliably in one step.`,
					`I split the job into smaller parts. Code handled the parts whose result could be fixed in advance. The language model was used later, when the product needed judgment to write a good definition or explanation. I have found the same pattern in agent workflows: make the state and available actions explicit, then ask the model to decide only where judgment is useful.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Let actual use overturn the original design',
				body: [
					`I liked the simplicity of Casamo’s original report. That didn’t make it the right design. Four people independently told me they wanted more options, and their reason exposed something I had missed about the decision they were making.`,
					`The important part of user feedback isn’t collecting a list of requested features. It is noticing when several people reveal the same mistaken assumption in the product. At that point, preserving the original idea matters less than changing it to fit how people use it.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Keep the product narrow while making its components reusable',
				body: [
					`A product can be specific without every part inside it being useful only once. The video tool can be designed around my way of making explanatory videos while still using general components for transcription, captions, screen recordings, and rendering.`,
					`I use the same approach with AI workflows. A scraper, a structured file, or a small browser tool can be reused in many systems. The product around those tools should still have a clear job. Reusable parts make the next product cheaper to build; a narrow product makes the current one easier to understand.`
				]
			}
		]
	},
	{
		slug: 'working-with-ai',
		title: 'Working with AI',
		purpose: 'How I design and operate reliable agent-assisted systems.',
		entries: [
			{
				title: 'Keep judgment in the loop',
				body: [
					`I learned this while building a LinkedIn workflow. Code could capture the 25 people on a search page, record their names, roles, companies, and whether the interface allowed a connection request. It couldn’t reliably decide which person I should contact based on my goals.`,
					`That last step needed judgment. The system became more reliable when code prepared the facts and an agent made the decision from them. I don’t try to remove intelligence from a task before I understand what the judgment involves.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Give agents small tools with clear jobs',
				body: [
					`An agent can research events by opening Meetup, clicking every result, reading each page, and deciding which events fit me. It works, but it is slow and gives the agent many chances to get lost.`,
					`I built a small tool that collects the event information into a text file instead. The agent can read the file and spend its effort comparing the events. A good agent tool doesn’t need to do the whole job. It removes a mechanical part that makes the agent’s real job harder.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Understand the workflow before automating it',
				body: [
					`My first instinct with a new task is often to ask the agent to do everything. After I watch the task happen several times, its internal steps become easier to see. Some steps can then become code, while others still need judgment.`,
					`This is why I treat early automation as observation as much as implementation. The first version teaches me what the task contains. Later versions can make the repeated parts faster without pretending I understood the workflow from the beginning.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'The human remains the executive',
				body: [
					`My voice-note workflow made this division clear to me. I speak through ideas, Codex turns them into tasks, an agent completes one of those tasks, and the result comes back to me. I review it, make a decision, and set the next direction.`,
					`The agent can plan and execute a great deal inside that loop. It still doesn’t decide what kind of life or business I am trying to build. My responsibility is to set goals, make decisions, and judge the information that comes back.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Agents need context and procedure',
				body: [
					`The quality of an agent’s work depends heavily on what it knows about the situation and what process it has been given. A general instruction leaves it to invent both. That can be useful while I am still discovering how a task should work, but it is a poor default for something repeated.`,
					`Once I understand the task, I preserve the relevant context in files and give the agent a procedure to follow. The context explains the situation. The procedure explains how to work within it.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Keep workflow boundaries narrow',
				body: [
					`Large instructions hide several different kinds of work inside one request. That was the problem when I asked a language model to create a Spanish story, use the right vocabulary and grammar, and write every definition and explanation at once.`,
					`I now separate a workflow into parts with clear inputs and outputs. The boundaries make failures easier to see and keep one bad result from contaminating the rest of the process. The agent gets freedom inside the part that needs judgment, not across the entire system.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Carry decisions into later tasks',
				body: [
					`A decision isn’t useful if the rest of the system continues from the old assumption. When I settled on a customer group for my consulting work, the tasks that depended on that choice needed to receive the new context.`,
					`I try to record decisions outside the conversation where they were made and update the work that follows from them. Otherwise every new agent starts by reopening settled questions or quietly makes its own version of the decision.`
				]
			}
		]
	},
	{
		slug: 'business-and-independent-work',
		title: 'Business and Independent Work',
		purpose: 'Lessons from trying to build an independent practice while allowing the offer and customer focus to change.',
		entries: [
			{
				title: 'Work on what changes the outcome',
				body: [
					`I can spend hours improving a product or an internal tool because that kind of work is comfortable for me. During a period when I needed a job or a consulting project, those improvements could feel productive while I avoided applications and conversations with potential clients.`,
					`I started stopping periodically to ask whether the last block of work changed my situation. The answer isn’t always to stop building. It is to be honest about whether the bottleneck is the product, finding clients, or delivering for the people already paying me.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Treat sales as the beginning of a relationship',
				body: [
					`I used to think too much about the perfect first message after someone accepted a LinkedIn connection. The mistake was assuming that the message needed to create a sale.`,
					`For consulting, the first interaction usually has a smaller job: begin a useful professional relationship. I can learn what someone is working on, pay attention to what they share, and help when I have something relevant to offer. A sales conversation can happen later if there is trust and a problem I can solve.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Diagnose before proposing a solution',
				body: [
					`When I meet someone, I am more interested in what they are working on and which parts of their business are difficult than in finding a quick opening for my service. Those questions tell me whether I understand the problem and whether I can help at all.`,
					`This changes the conversation from a disguised pitch into research and service. Sometimes the right response is advice, an introduction, or nothing. If there is a project, the proposal begins from the person’s situation rather than from a solution I arrived wanting to sell.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'A narrow customer group creates knowledge that compounds',
				body: [
					`Relationship-based sales takes time. Trying to understand several unrelated kinds of businesses means starting from zero in each conversation.`,
					`A narrower customer group lets me keep learning the same processes, language, and recurring problems. Each conversation improves the next one, and I become more capable of offering something useful before asking for anything. The current customer group may change; the value of accumulated understanding does not.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Remove rules that do not change what you do next',
				body: [
					`I spent time trying to define exactly when I should use LinkedIn and when I should use email. The distinction produced more planning without changing the basic work: choose a channel, find people I can help, and start conversations.`,
					`A framework is useful when it makes the next action clearer. If it creates categories I have to maintain but leads to the same action, I can remove it. This has made independent work feel less like designing a perfect business in advance and more like doing the few things the business needs.`
				]
			}
		]
	},
	{
		slug: 'learning-and-research',
		title: 'Learning and Research',
		purpose: 'How I try to turn information into usable judgment.',
		entries: [
			{
				title: 'Start with the capability, not the reading list',
				body: [
					`I once tried to design a curriculum for becoming a better technical consultant. Before I had started the reading, it had expanded into a boot camp for one potential customer group. The plan had become more specific than the work I had evidence I needed to do.`,
					`That made me question the starting point. It is easier to choose what to study after naming what I want to be able to do: understand a business process, run a better client conversation, design an AI workflow, or deliver a particular kind of project. The reading list should serve the capability.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Explain ideas aloud to test your understanding',
				body: [
					`When I finish a chapter, podcast, or experiment, I can explain it during a walk. Speaking forces me to reconstruct the idea without leaning on the source’s wording. The unclear parts become obvious because I can’t explain why one point leads to the next.`,
					`The recording also gives me something usable afterward. It can become a note, a question to research, or the beginning of a piece of writing. The explanation is both a check on my understanding and a way to preserve what I learned.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Learn technical work through projects',
				body: [
					`When I find an open-source tool that might help with a video workflow, I want to inspect the code, understand how it works, and then use the mechanism in a project of my own. The project gives the learning a concrete test: either I can make the workflow work or I still have something to understand.`,
					`This is how much of my technical learning happens. A problem gives me a reason to study a tool or concept, and building with it reveals the parts a general explanation would let me skim past.`
				]
			}
		]
	},
	{
		slug: 'writing-and-communication',
		title: 'Writing and Communication',
		purpose: 'How I find, develop, and communicate ideas.',
		entries: [
			{
				title: 'Speak before judging the thought',
				body: [
					`During my first walk-and-talk recordings, I noticed myself going silent because I had decided the thought in my head wasn’t worth capturing. There was nothing at stake, but I was still filtering the idea before I had heard it.`,
					`I began treating continued speech as part of the exercise. Most thoughts won’t become something public, and they don’t need to. Speaking first gives the worthwhile ones enough time to take shape before I decide what they are.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Walking and talking is rehearsal',
				body: [
					`Talking during walks has made it easier for me to explain an idea for longer without stopping. It has also made me more comfortable continuing when someone walks past and can hear me speaking English to myself in an Argentine park.`,
					`That practice feels connected to other forms of communication. Sitting in front of a camera is still different, but it no longer begins from silence. I have already spent time finding the thought, saying it badly, and learning how I would say it again.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Separate creating, editing, and implementing',
				body: [
					`The walk gives me a place to create without trying to finish anything. Later, I can review the transcript, decide which ideas are worth keeping, and turn those decisions into writing or tasks.`,
					`I think of these as separate modes: create, edit, and implement. Mixing them makes me judge an idea while it is forming or start building every idea before I have compared it with the others. Separation lets each mode do one job.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Test an idea before expanding it',
				body: [
					`After a few productive walks, it was tempting to design an entire system around them. I still wasn’t sure whether the useful part was walking, speaking aloud, or both.`,
					`The next step was a smaller experiment: try speaking while sitting down and see whether the ideas developed in the same way. A question like that is better answered by another recording than by adding more machinery to the system.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Use interviews when the source material is too thin',
				body: [
					`A voice note or atomic note can identify a subject without containing enough detail for public writing. It may state the conclusion but leave out what happened, why I cared, or what changed my mind.`,
					`My proposed answer is a short interview after choosing the topic. The questions should collect the missing experience and reasoning before any draft is written. That keeps the finished piece connected to what I think instead of asking an AI to fill the gaps with a plausible version of me.`
				]
			}
		]
	},
	{
		slug: 'systems-and-attention',
		title: 'Systems and Attention',
		purpose: 'How I manage decisions, context, and limited attention.',
		entries: [
			{
				title: 'Keep decisions settled until something changes',
				body: [
					`Having many interests can make every choice feel like the permanent rejection of all the others. I have become better at choosing one direction by treating the decision as current rather than eternal.`,
					`Once I decide, I try not to reopen the question unless the situation changes or new evidence gives me a reason. This protects the time after a decision from becoming another round of comparison while leaving room to change my mind when the original assumptions stop being true.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Prefer simple frameworks that affect the next action',
				body: [
					`I have a tendency to overthink systems, and an AI can multiply that tendency by producing an elaborate answer to every distinction I invent. I saw this when I tried to create rules for choosing between LinkedIn and email even though both led to the same immediate work.`,
					`The frameworks that help me most reduce the number of choices. Dividing consulting into getting work and delivering work was useful because it showed where my attention was needed. A framework earns its place when it changes what I do next.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Choose a work rhythm that fits the person and season',
				body: [
					`Some people work steadily and others move through intense periods. I don’t think one rhythm is correct for everyone. I tend to follow subjects that absorb me, and the amount of attention I give them changes over time.`,
					`The practical question is whether the rhythm still serves the life I am trying to build. That answer can change with my responsibilities, energy, and goals. I have to learn it through experience rather than forcing myself into a schedule because it works for someone else.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Preserve decisions and context for future work',
				body: [
					`In an agent-assisted system, a useful decision can disappear inside the conversation where it was made. The next task then begins without knowing what was settled and may repeat the research or move in another direction.`,
					`I use Markdown files to keep the goals, decisions, and information that later work depends on. The files don’t make the decisions for me. They let me and the agents continue from the same situation instead of rebuilding it from memory each time.`
				]
			}
		]
	},
	{
		slug: 'relationships-and-community',
		title: 'Relationships and Community',
		purpose: 'The place of other people in my professional and personal values.',
		entries: [
			{
				title: 'Community is a value, not a networking strategy',
				body: [
					`Community affects the kind of person I want to be. I want to be useful to individual people and to the groups I am part of, whether that means giving advice to someone younger, organizing an event, supporting my family, or helping a business operate better.`,
					`Some of those relationships may lead to work, but that isn’t what makes them valuable. If I treated community only as a source of introductions and clients, I would be reducing the people around me to a business channel.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Money can be a tool; people cannot',
				body: [
					`I grew up around the idea that money itself was bad and that rich people were bad people. My view changed as I got older. Money is a tool, and the problem begins when it becomes the highest value rather than something used in service of other values.`,
					`People belong in a different category. They aren’t instruments for reaching my goals. A decision about work, moving, or money also has to account for the people who rely on me and the effect the decision will have on their lives.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Begin professional relationships with curiosity and usefulness',
				body: [
					`When I first thought about LinkedIn outreach, I was looking for a first message that could lead quickly to a sale. I later realized that the better starting point was to understand the person: what they are building, which problems they see, and whether I have anything useful to contribute.`,
					`That might mean sharing an idea, making an introduction, or simply paying attention until I understand their work better. It also lets me choose professional relationships around values such as thoughtfulness, ambition, craft, honesty, and service rather than treating every possible buyer as interchangeable.`
				]
			}
		]
	},
	{
		slug: 'health-and-training',
		title: 'Health and Training',
		purpose: 'What long-term strength training has taught me about goals, adaptation, and changing intensity.',
		entries: [
			{
				title: 'Strength training as a long-term identity',
				body: [
					`I became obsessed with strength training when I was about fifteen. For roughly ten years, I trained hard and built toward numbers that still define my personal records: a 500-pound squat, 405-pound bench press, 620-pound deadlift, and 225-pound press.`,
					`The intensity changed after that period, but training never disappeared. I kept doing it because it was important to me and because I liked how it made me feel. That continuity matters more to me than pretending I have approached it with the same ambition every year.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Simplify training around the goal',
				body: [
					`My current goal is clear: break my old personal records. That made it easier to remove exercises that didn’t have much to do with the goal and organize the week around the four lifts I want to improve.`,
					`I use 5/3/1 loading for the main lifts, follow most of them with First Set Last work, and keep the rest small: rows or chin-ups for my back, split squats, and direct core work. The program is easier to follow because every exercise has an obvious reason to be there.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'More work is not always more progress',
				body: [
					`I used to include more assistance work because there was always another exercise that seemed useful. But adding useful exercises one by one can leave you with a program that no longer fits its main purpose.`,
					`Removing exercises wasn’t a decision to care less about training. It let me put more of my time and recovery into the lifts I am trying to improve. If the goal changes, the program can change with it. For this period, more work would mostly make the target less clear.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Training intensity moves through seasons',
				body: [
					`After I reached my old records, continuing at the same rate would have required more time and effort than I wanted to give it. I still trained and stayed strong, but I stopped organizing as much of my life around pushing the numbers higher.`,
					`Years later, I want to push again. I don’t see the quieter period as quitting or the renewed goal as starting over. Training stayed part of my life at a lower intensity until I had the desire and space to make it a larger goal again.`
				]
			}
		]
	},
	{
		slug: 'life-philosophy',
		title: 'Life Philosophy',
		purpose: 'The values and open questions connecting the rest of this library.',
		entries: [
			{
				title: 'The values currently guiding my life',
				body: [
					`When I recently tried to name the values guiding me, I arrived at truth, ambition, simplicity, and community. I call them current values deliberately. Writing them down helps me compare a decision with the kind of person I am trying to become, but I don’t assume I have finished understanding myself.`,
					`The values also correct one another. Ambition without community can become selfish. Community without truth can become dishonest agreement. Simplicity helps me act, but it can’t be an excuse to ignore a difficult fact.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Money is a tool for expressing values',
				body: [
					`Money matters to me because of what it allows me to do. It can create security, give me more control over my time, support my girlfriend and my mother, and help me build things that are useful to other people.`,
					`That makes money important without putting it at the top of my hierarchy. If I make it the final goal, it stops answering the more important question of what the money is for and what kind of life earning it is meant to support.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'Make decisions without pretending they are permanent',
				body: [
					`I have struggled with choosing among several interests because committing to one means setting the others aside. The only way forward is still to choose.`,
					`I try to make a decision and stop reconsidering it until the situation changes. That doesn’t require me to pretend the choice will define the rest of my life. It gives the choice enough time to teach me something while preserving my ability to respond to new evidence.`
				]
			},
			{
				title: 'A person’s way of working and living can change over time',
				body: [
					`I don’t think there is one final answer to how a person should organize work, ambition, relationships, and rest. The answer depends on the person’s disposition and can change as responsibilities and interests change.`,
					`The self is something I uncover through experience. A future vision can give me direction, but I can’t cling to the exact picture as though I will remain the same person on the way there. Trying to live an intentional life means continuing to ask what serves me now, my future self, and the people around me.`
				]
			}
		]
	}
];

---

<BaseLayout
	title={`${title} | Hanif Carroll`}
	description={description}
	bodyClass='hc-projects-archive hc-notes'
	showHeader={false}
	showFooterCta={false}
	showFooter={false}
>
	<div class='hc-archive-shell'>
		<details class='hc-notes-mobile-menu'>
			<summary>
				<span>Notes</span>
				<span class='hc-notes-mobile-menu__action'><span class='hc-notes-mobile-menu__open'>Table of Contents</span><span class='hc-notes-mobile-menu__close'>Close</span><svg viewBox='0 0 24 24' width='16' height='16' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' stroke-width='2' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' aria-hidden='true'><path d='M6 9l6 6 6-6'></path></svg></span>
			</summary>
			<div class='hc-notes-mobile-menu__panel'>
				<p class='hc-notes-toc-label'>Chapters</p>
				<nav aria-label='Mobile table of contents'>
					<ol class='hc-notes-mobile-toc'>
						{sections.map((section, sectionIndex) => (
							<li>
								<details class='hc-notes-mobile-section' open={sectionIndex === 0}>
									<summary><strong>{section.title}</strong><svg viewBox='0 0 24 24' width='18' height='18' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' stroke-width='2' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' aria-hidden='true'><path d='M6 9l6 6 6-6'></path></svg></summary>
									<ol>
										{section.entries.map((entry) => (
											<li><a href={`#${entryId(section.slug, entry.title)}`}>{entry.title}</a></li>
										))}
									</ol>
								</details>
							</li>
						))}
					</ol>
				</nav>
			</div>
		</details>

		<header class='hc-archive-header hc-notes-sidebar'>
			<div class='hc-notes-sidebar__brand'>
				<figure><Image src={Portrait} alt='Hanif Carroll' loading='eager' widths={[96, 192]} sizes='48px' /></figure>
				<div><a href='/'>Hanif Carroll</a><span>Notes</span></div>
			</div>

			<div class='hc-notes-sidebar__toc'>
				<p class='hc-notes-toc-label'>Table of contents</p>
				<nav class='hc-archive-nav' aria-label='Table of contents'>
					<ul>
						{sections.map((section, sectionIndex) => (
							<li class='hc-notes-nav-section' data-notes-section>
								<a href={`#${section.slug}`} data-notes-section-link>
									<span class='hc-notes-nav-number' aria-hidden='true'>{String(sectionIndex + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
									<span class='hc-archive-nav__text'>{section.title}</span>
								</a>
								<ol>
									{section.entries.map((entry) => (
										<li><a class='hc-notes-nav-entry' href={`#${entryId(section.slug, entry.title)}`}>{entry.title}</a></li>
									))}
								</ol>
							</li>
						))}
					</ul>
				</nav>
			</div>

			<div class='hc-archive-header__footer'>
				<div class='hc-archive-header__actions'>
					<a
						class='hc-archive-resume'
						href='/Hanif-Carroll-Resume.pdf'
						download
						data-track-event='resume_downloaded'
						data-track-location='notes_rail'
					>
						Download résumé
					</a>
					<a
						class='hc-archive-inquiry'
						href={BOOK_CALL_URL}
						target='_blank'
						rel='noopener noreferrer'
						data-track-event='book_call_clicked'
						data-track-location='notes_rail'
					>
						Book a call <span aria-hidden='true'>&nearr;</span>
					</a>
				</div>

				<ul class='hc-archive-social' aria-label='Social media and contact'>
					<li>
						<a href='https://github.com/hanifcarroll' target='_blank' rel='noopener noreferrer' aria-label='GitHub (opens in a new tab)' title='GitHub'>
